{
  "site_name": "RogueForge",
  "site_tagline": "The Rogue Restoration Project",
  "site_description": "RogueForge, the Rogue Restoration Project, preserves Rogue and its descendants. In 2026 it started over from first principles, rebuilding early versions of Rogue until their source compiles to the same bytes as the original binaries.",
  "github_href": "https://github.com/rogueforge",
  "headline": "Rogue Restoration Project",
  "lede": "<p>In 2026 we went back to first principles: the three source trees that survive close to their releases (Rogue 3.6, 5.2 and 5.4), and the original programs preserved in Berkeley's BSD distribution trees. Where a binary survives, a version is done when its source compiles back into it.</p><p>Claude, Anthropic&rsquo;s AI model, did the heavy lifting, reading PDP-11 and VAX machine code one function at a time and rewriting C until the compilers of the day produced the very same bytes. Six versions so far. Nothing counted until it recompiled.</p>",
